.TH FTREE 1
.SH NAME
ftree \- file tree and name space browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/ftree
[
.B -n
] [
.B -d
] [
.B -e
|
.B -E
|
.B -p
] [
.I root
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ftree
shows the given
.I root
directory
(default:
.BR / )
as a tree: the files and folders of a folder are listed beneath it,
indented, folders first.
A folder is shown closed,
.BR ▸ ,
until opened, when its mark is
.B ▾
and what it holds is listed under it.
Clicking button 1 on the mark, or double-clicking the name, opens or
closes a folder; double-clicking a file, or button 2, opens it.
A file opens through the
.IR plumber (8),
so in Lucifer it lands in the presentation view, an editor for text and
a viewer for images and documents; with no plumber, it is put in
Lucifer's presentation view directly.
.PP
Button 3 pops up a menu of operations on the selected file or folder:
.BR open ;
.BR copy ,
which remembers it, and
.BR "paste into" ,
which copies what was remembered, a folder whole, into the selected
folder;
.BR remove... ,
which asks, then removes it and all it holds;
.BR "bind here..." ,
which asks for a source and flags
.RB ( -b ,
.BR -a ,
.BR -c )
and binds it on the selected folder (or a file's folder), and
.BR unmount ;
.B "expand all"
and
.BR "collapse all" ;
.BR refresh ,
which reads the tree again keeping what is open open;
.BR "ns mode" ,
which shows, beside each mount point, what is bound or mounted there,
its flags and how many layers a union has;
.BR goto ,
which shows another folder as the root, or opens a file; and
.BR exit .
.PP
The arrow keys move the selection; Right or Enter opens, Left closes
or goes to the folder above; Page Up, Page Down, Home and End move
further.
Ctrl-B binds, Ctrl-U unmounts, Ctrl-G goes to a path, Ctrl-N switches ns
mode, r refreshes and q or Ctrl-Q quits.
.PP
.I Ftree
runs in a name space of its own: what it binds and unmounts changes
what it shows, not the name space of the program that started it.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B -n
Start in ns mode.
.TP
.B -d
Allow only opening: no copy, paste, remove, bind or unmount.
.TP
.B -e
Ignore `exit' but offer move and resize.
.TP
.B -E
Ignore exit, and do not offer move or resize.
.TP
.B -p
Do exit, but do not offer move or resize.
.PP
The last three make a user interface for handheld touch screens: a
start panel is
.I ftree
with
.BR -E ,
always there, the rest of the interaction made by the name space under
.I root
and the plumbing rules.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/veltro/ftree/state
.TP
.B /tmp/veltro/ftree/view
What is shown, for an agent: the root, the selection and the rows on
screen.
.TP
.B /tmp/veltro/ftree/ctl
Commands from an agent:
.BI cd " path" \fR,
.BI select " path" \fR,
.BR expand ,
.BR collapse ,
.BR refresh ,
.BR nsmode ,
.BI bind " src target" " \fR[\fPflags\fR]" \fR,
.BI unmount " path" \fR,
.B scroll
.BR up | down | top | bottom .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/ftree.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/ftree/cptree.b
